    [Feature][Build] Limit local csrc cache versions per operator (#18793)
    
    ### What this PR does / why we need it?
    
    Bound local L1 history to two `operator_text_hash` directories per
    SOC/operator by default, retaining all action entries inside each
    version.
    
    - The retention count is a **script-local**
    `BUILD_CACHE_MAX_OPERATOR_VERSIONS=2` beside `BUILD_CACHE_DIR` in
    `csrc/build.sh`. There is no new project-level environment variable.
    - `setup.py` and `vllm_ascend/envs.py` are unchanged. Cleanup belongs to
    successful **csrc source compilation/package generation**, not later
    Python packaging or installation.
    - A third hash may coexist while compiling. Cleanup runs sequentially at
    the end of `build.sh`, after all foreground csrc build commands and
    their parallel compilation tasks succeed. There is no new build/cleanup
    guard or EXIT handler. Failed builds do not reach cleanup; cleanup
    errors do not fail the successful build. Concurrent builds must not
    share a directory being pruned.
    - HIT/SAVE refresh version recency. Compaction marks `.updated` for the
    next trusted L1 snapshot save. Published build outputs are not removed.
    - Consolidate repeated prepared-input hash/manifest recording and the
    common uncached-build flow. Merge duplicate marker/cold-warm tests,
    parameterize recipe/environment and publication-lock tests, remove the
    superseded cleanup-guard-only coverage, and remove the superseded
    package-level configuration tests.
    
    Schema remains **4**. Snapshot/action identity, manifests, artifact
    ownership and existing publication locks are unchanged. No workflow
    changes or nightly environment adaptation are included.
    
    This bounds local operator source-version groups, not total bytes,
    third-party entries, action variants inside a retained version, or
    historical OBS snapshots.
    
    ### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?
    
    Successful `csrc/build.sh` builds retain two source hashes per
    SOC/operator. Adjust the local `BUILD_CACHE_MAX_OPERATOR_VERSIONS` value
    in that script to change the count; nonpositive values disable cleanup.
    There is no package-level configuration.
    
    Direct CMake/action users may explicitly run `python3
    csrc/scripts/build_cache.py prune --cache-root <cache-dir>
    --max-operator-versions 2` after a successful batch. Cleanup is
    best-effort, so failed builds or cleanup errors can temporarily retain
    more versions. Direct callers must wait for all builds using that cache
    directory to finish before pruning.
    
    ### How was this patch tested?
    
    #### Tool and shell regression tests
    
    ```bash
    python3 -m pytest --confcutdir=tests/ut/_tools \
      tests/ut/_tools/test_build_cache.py \
      tests/ut/_tools/test_build_cache_concurrency.py \
      tests/ut/_tools/test_prepare_csrc_l1_restore.py -q -rs
    ```
    
    - Isolated Linux aarch64/Python 3.12.13 container with CMake: **103
    passed**, no skips.
    - Covers whole-hash retention, multiple actions per hash, HIT-only
    compaction, current-version recency, deletion and symlink safety, and
    the actual trailing shell cleanup block after parallel child processes
    finish, with successful/failed builds and 0/1/2/3/invalid limits.
    - File-scoped manual pre-commit hooks (including shellcheck and
    markdownlint) and `git diff --check`: passed. No workflow files change;
    actionlint was skipped.
    
    #### Real partial AscendC compilation
    
    In an isolated aarch64/CANN 9.2.0 environment, without NPU devices:
    
    ```bash
    cd csrc
    bash build.sh --pkg --ops=copy_and_expand_eagle_inputs --soc=ascend910_93 -j4
    ```
    
    Only `copy_and_expand_eagle_inputs` was selected. The compatible
    protobuf L1 entry was seeded from the same image to avoid unrelated
    repeated third-party compilation; **the selected operator cache started
    empty**. Already downloaded third-party sources were also reused after
    an initial dependency-download attempt failed on DNS resolution. Each
    successful invocation used a fresh native build directory and retained
    L1; the operator entries were not preseeded.
    
    | Case | Custom-operator inner telemetry | csrc build wall time |
    Retained source hashes |
    | --- | --- | ---: | ---: |
    | Operator cold | MISS=1, SAVED=1 | 18.440 s | 1 |
    | Same-source warm | HIT=1, MISS=0 | 14.735 s | 1 |
    | Source hash B | MISS=1, SAVED=1 | 18.038 s | 2 |
    | Source hash C | MISS=1, SAVED=1 | 17.994 s | 2 |
    
    B/C were harmless text-only source mutations used to exercise distinct
    operator hashes, not kernel algorithm changes. Every case generated a
    real `.run` installer. The third version's log shows successful package
    generation **before** `PRUNED`; the oldest hash was removed and the two
    newer hashes remained.
    
    These are single-operator observations, not a full-project speedup
    claim. The initial test above was single-operator only; it is superseded
    by the full-project validation below.
    
    #### Full-project validation of `068aa06e0` (2026-10-15)
    
    Validated in a task-isolated Linux aarch64 / CANN 9.2.0 / Python 3.12.13
    / PyTorch 2.10.0 / torch_npu 2.10.0.post4 environment on an actual A3
    NPU. Source was exported from the exact commit; the build script and
    cache engine hashes matched the PR. No default A3 operator was excluded:
    **43 selected operators, 170 custom-operator cache actions**.
    
    Normal complete editable installation was used, including the custom-op
    .run package, its installation, the native kernels shared library and
    the PyTorch extension:
    
    ```bash
    COMPILE_CUSTOM_KERNELS=1 MAX_JOBS=16 \
      python3 -m pip install -e . --no-build-isolation --no-deps -v
    ```
    
    Dependencies were already installed in the image; third-party source
    downloads were provided from that same image. The cold L1 was empty,
    including the protobuf entry. This tests the project native
    build/install pipeline, not fresh dependency resolution.
    
    | Case | Custom-operator telemetry | Complete install time | Tested
    operator source hashes retained |
    | --- | --- | ---: | ---: |
    | Cold | MISS=170, SAVED=170 | 678.152 s | 1 |
    | Warm, fresh csrc build directory | HIT=170, MISS=0 | 104.419 s | 1 |
    | Source version B | HIT=169, MISS=1, SAVED=1 | 96.118 s | 2 |
    | Source version C | HIT=169, MISS=1, SAVED=1 | 96.431 s | 2 |
    
    The third-party action was MISS/SAVED once on cold and HIT once on the
    fresh-directory warm run. B/C are comment-only changes to
    `copy_and_expand_eagle_inputs`, not kernel algorithm changes. Version C
    was observed with **three** hash directories during construction; its
    log shows CPack package generation before `PRUNED`, then the old A hash
    was absent and B/C remained. These are one-environment observations, not
    a whole-matrix performance claim.
    
    Real-script fault injection also passed after restoring the real A cache
    entry to recreate an existing three-version history:
    
    - CMake build injected exit 23: nonzero propagated, all three hashes
    retained, no prune.
    - Unwritable-by-construction update marker (dangling target) during
    cleanup: warning, successful csrc build and operator-package
    installation retained, all three hashes retained.
    - Restore the normal marker and run the normal csrc build again:
    automatic pruning recovered to two hashes, oldest A removed, `.updated`
    present.
    
    Real NPU regression of the newly installed extension: **225 passed**
    after cold installation and **225 passed** again after version
    eviction/fault recovery, covering the existing CopyAndExpandEagleInputs,
    RMSNormCast (including NPU graph) and AddRmsNormBias tests. Package,
    extension and vendor paths were checked to belong to the candidate, not
    image-preinstalled vLLM-Ascend. The first task-harness attempt
    initialized the device before custom-op bootstrap and failed tiling
    lookup; fixing that harness ordering to match the existing
    `enable_custom_op()` contract resolved it without project-code changes.
    
    Scope: native build/install and selected real custom-op execution on one
    A3 environment; no model-serving or multi-device/multi-environment
    acceptance, no new private-repository CI run. Latest upstream
    pre-commit/mypy and both CPU UT jobs passed; precision-test CI still
    requires a maintainer's run-enabling label.
    
    ### Checklist
    
    #### Testing
    
    - [x] **Boundary / edge-case coverage**: 0/1/2/3/invalid limits,
    third-version coexistence and whole-version retention.
    - [x] **Regression test**: oversize restored histories and delayed
    cleanup are covered.
    - [x] **Error / failure path coverage**: source-build failure, existing
    entry/action/publish lock failures, deletion errors and symlinks.
    - [x] **Meaningful & reliable tests**: real engine subprocesses, actual
    trailing shell cleanup, and real partial AscendC compilation/package
    generation.
    - [ ] **Performance impact**: partial-build observations above; no
    full-project performance regression benchmark.
    
